## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-cloud multi-tenant infra from a single module, balancing reuse with isolation. Targets provider aliasing, per-tenant tagging, and policy-driven gates.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliasing and for_each
- Cross-cloud module composition
- Policy-as-code (OPA/Vault) for prod changes
- CI gates around per-tenant plan validation

## Code Example

```hcl
# skeleton: tenants map and module per-tenant
variable "tenants" {
  type = map(object({
    cloud  = string
    region = string
  }))
}

module "tenant" {
  for_each = var.tenants
  source   = "./modules/tenant"

  tenant_name = each.key
  cloud       = each.value.cloud
  region      = each.value.region

  # pass per-tenant providers (pseudo-syntax for illustration)
  providers = {
    aws    = aws.${each.value.cloud == "aws" ? "alias" : "default"}
    google = google.${each.value.cloud == "gcp" ? "alias" : "default"}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test drift with cross-cloud resources and ensure idempotence? 
- What are trade-offs between centralized tenants state vs per-tenant backends?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to structure a tiny Terraform project with proper state backends, a reusable module, and basic CI guards.

## Key Concepts
- Local vs remote backends and state isolation
- Modules and outputs for reusability
- State locking with DynamoDB
- CI gates that validate plan scope

## Code Example
```hcl
# backend for dev (local)
terraform {
  backend "local" {
    path = "terraform.tfstate"
  }
}
```

```hcl
# modules/storage/main.tf
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = "private"
  versioning {
    enabled = var.versioning
  }
}

resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "lock" {
  name           = "tfstate-lock"
  billing_mode   = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
  hash_key       = "LockID"
  attribute {
    name = "LockID"
    type = "S"
  }
}

output "bucket_name" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.bucket.bucket
}

output "locks_table_arn" {
  value = aws_dynamodb_table.lock.arn
}
```

```hcl
# modules/storage/variables.tf
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool; default = true }
```

```hcl
# backend for prod (example)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "tfstate-prod"
    key            = "path/to/env/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tfstate-lock"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend the module to support versioned backups with lifecycle rules?
- How would you add a simple test to ensure only module-controlled attributes change during plan?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-repo data sharing and multi-region provider patterns, plus a practical gating approach to prevent drift.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliasing across regions
- data.terraform_remote_state for read-only cross-repo data
- Per-region backends and workflows
- CI policy gates to enforce drift checks and approvals
- Cross-region resource referencing of VPC/subnet IDs

## Code Example
```javascript
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.3"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "east"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "west"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
data "terraform_remote_state" "net" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "infra-network-bucket"
    key    = "network/us-east-1/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
# usage
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  provider    = aws.west
  ami         = "ami-0abcdef1234567890"
  subnet_id   = data.terraform_remote_state.net.outputs.public_subnet_id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection across repos? 
- How would you handle regional failover and state restoration?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform design, policy-as-code, drift handling, and test automation at scale—core for Oracle, Tesla, and LinkedIn-like environments.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliasing for multi-region/multi-cloud setups
- Wrapper modules to standardize tagging and policy constraints
- Terraform Cloud/Sentinel or OPA for governance
- Drift detection workflows and isolated drift environments
- End-to-end testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example

```hcl
# Wrapper module injecting standard tags
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
variable "tags" { type = map(string) }

module "service" {
  source = "../aws-azure-base"
  providers = {
    aws   = aws.us_east_1
    aws_eu = aws.eu_west_1
    azurerm = azurerm.westus2
  }
  tags = merge(var.tags, {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you structure a Sentinel policy to enforce tag requirements and deny secrets in state?
- How would you simulate drift in tests and verify policy compliance across clouds?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design multi-cloud Terraform architectures with per-tenant isolation, cross-cloud networking, and automated post-deployment validation including drift detection and reporting.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant Terraform Cloud workspaces with unique workspace keys
- Provider-agnostic networking module (AWS VPC, GCP VNet)
- Policy-as-code enforcement (OPA/Sentinel)
- Drift detection running after apply
- CI gates for tenant-scoped plans

## Code Example

```python
def detect_drift(desired_state, live_state):
    drift = {}
    for resource_id, desired_config in desired_state.items():
        live_config = live_state.get(resource_id)
        if not live_config:
            drift[resource_id] = "missing"
        elif live_config != desired_config:
            drift[resource_id] = {
                "expected": desired_config,
                "actual": live_config
            }
    return drift
```
